WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.972 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:00.972 --> 00:00:01.944 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:01.944 --> 00:00:12.460 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.760 align:middle line:84%
JAKE XIA: So in my part
of the first lecture,

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.560 align:middle line:84%
I will, before I get
into the introduction

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.580 align:middle line:84%
of some financial concepts
and investment strategies,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.240 align:middle line:84%
give you some basics
on the background

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.480 align:middle line:90%
information for this class.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.920 align:middle line:84%
I will tell you three
things first before that.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.760 align:middle line:84%
First is a bit of
introduction of myself.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.340 align:middle line:84%
Second, I tell you how this
class was actually formed,

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:44.000 align:middle line:84%
why we had this idea, why we
decided to have this class.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.160 align:middle line:84%
Third is, before you
actually do the assignment 0,

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.440 align:middle line:84%
submit your
information, I'm going

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.540 align:middle line:84%
to survey the
class a little bit,

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.160 align:middle line:84%
just get some idea
of your background,

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.500 align:middle line:84%
of the knowledge of
financial markets and math,

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.890 align:middle line:84%
so we know what level
we should target you at.

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:09.930 align:middle line:84%
So first of all, me, so my
connection with MIT, as Vasili

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.010 align:middle line:90%
said, also quite similar.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:16.130 align:middle line:84%
So I have a PhD from Core
6, electrical engineering

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:18.010 align:middle line:90%
and computer science.

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:21.530 align:middle line:84%
And I worked in the
engineering field

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:26.130 align:middle line:84%
as a researcher for a
few years, then switched

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.210 align:middle line:90%
to Wall Street as a quant.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:34.210 align:middle line:84%
Quant means using mathematics,
building trading models.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.690 align:middle line:84%
Back then, I joined
Salomon Brothers.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:40.830 align:middle line:84%
So that tells you about
my age because probably--

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:44.250 align:middle line:84%
how many of you have heard
about Salomon Brothers?

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:45.190 align:middle line:90%
Oh, good for you.

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:48.650 align:middle line:90%
It's now part of Citigroup.

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:51.330 align:middle line:84%
But Salomon Brothers,
back then, was the pioneer

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.770 align:middle line:84%
of using quantitative
trading strategies

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:58.410 align:middle line:84%
and also hiring
mathematicians, of doing

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.530 align:middle line:84%
those so-called
arbitrage models,

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:06.930 align:middle line:84%
finding one stock or
bond versus another,

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:12.130 align:middle line:84%
basically finding
relative value.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:15.570 align:middle line:84%
So I worked there for a
couple of years as a quant.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:20.570 align:middle line:84%
Then Morgan Stanley recruited
me away to be a trader.

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:24.650 align:middle line:84%
I ended up staying there
for over 17 years, worked

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:29.790 align:middle line:84%
as various trading and trading
management roles globally,

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.450 align:middle line:90%
got sent around the globe.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:37.530 align:middle line:84%
That's where I
worked with Vasili.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:43.770 align:middle line:84%
And in 2013-- that was the
year when we first recorded

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:47.890 align:middle line:84%
our OpenCourseWare
video for this class--

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:52.010 align:middle line:84%
I actually switched my
job from Morgan Stanley

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:55.010 align:middle line:84%
to Harvard Management
Company, which

00:02:55.010 --> 00:03:00.260 align:middle line:84%
is the entity that manages
Harvard University's endowment.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:03.820 align:middle line:84%
I joined as the
chief risk officer

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:08.540 align:middle line:84%
to oversee risk management
and also capital allocation.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:15.460 align:middle line:84%
That's when, back then, we had
many internal fund managers

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.900 align:middle line:90%
doing various strategies.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:23.040 align:middle line:84%
We, since then, changed our
model, about 7 years ago,

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.140 align:middle line:84%
7 or 8 years ago, to focus on
investing with external funds.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:32.800 align:middle line:84%
I subsequently also
switched my focus full time,

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.100 align:middle line:84%
focusing on
investing and finding

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.500 align:middle line:90%
the right funds and managers.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.480 align:middle line:84%
I'll tell you a bit
more about that.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.300 align:middle line:90%
But I will save that for later.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:46.580 align:middle line:90%
So that's basically about me.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:49.220 align:middle line:84%
So I'll tell you a
bit about the class.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:57.070 align:middle line:84%
So back, I want to say, maybe
14 or 15 years ago, when

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:00.170 align:middle line:84%
Vasili and I were working
together at Morgan Stanley,

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:04.710 align:middle line:84%
we came to MIT to
recruit quite often.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.290 align:middle line:84%
And Vasili was from
the math department.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.310 align:middle line:90%
I was from Core 6.

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:14.030 align:middle line:84%
So we met a lot of
really bright students

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.149 align:middle line:90%
with quantitative background.

00:04:16.149 --> 00:04:19.589 align:middle line:84%
But they don't really
know much about finance.

00:04:19.589 --> 00:04:23.110 align:middle line:84%
And they ask many
good questions.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:25.750 align:middle line:84%
So we just felt like,
in a half an hour

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:30.710 align:middle line:84%
or one hour interview time slot,
it's simply not enough for us

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:34.510 align:middle line:84%
to answer all the questions
or get to know the students.

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:36.590 align:middle line:84%
So we figured maybe
the best way is

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:41.310 align:middle line:84%
to have a seminar to tell
the students more about how

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.670 align:middle line:90%
math is used in finance.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:49.110 align:middle line:84%
So one seminar lecture actually
got expanded to the whole term.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.950 align:middle line:84%
And so we invited
different practitioners

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:57.570 align:middle line:84%
talking about how methods being
applied in different fields,

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.630 align:middle line:84%
solving different problems
that the class actually

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.550 align:middle line:90%
got very popular.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:05.430 align:middle line:84%
I mean, one of
the first classes,

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.990 align:middle line:84%
we've got students sit
on the floor everywhere.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:11.470 align:middle line:84%
They want to know
about should they

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:15.230 align:middle line:84%
pursue a career in
quantitative finance?

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:19.870 align:middle line:84%
So the math department
was very supportive.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:23.910 align:middle line:84%
And so we teamed up with
Peter the following year

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:27.590 align:middle line:84%
to offer half of the
lectures in actually math

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:31.030 align:middle line:84%
to fill up all the
background knowledge,

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:35.750 align:middle line:84%
and then kept the half
of the class talking

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:38.190 align:middle line:90%
about application cases.

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:42.150 align:middle line:84%
We even had field trips taking
all the students to New York,

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:45.710 align:middle line:84%
touring the trading
floor of Morgan Stanley.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:47.590 align:middle line:90%
We did that for a few years.

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:51.650 align:middle line:90%
And we had various fun.

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.180 align:middle line:84%
And some students really
ended up working in New York.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:59.440 align:middle line:84%
Some of them are still
there in various jobs.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:02.680 align:middle line:84%
So that's how this
class came about.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:06.000 align:middle line:84%
So let me turn the
table around, asking you

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.680 align:middle line:90%
a few quick questions.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.100 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to
state your name.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.060 align:middle line:90%
Just raise your hands.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:10.840 align:middle line:90%
It's fine.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:14.680 align:middle line:84%
How many of you have watched
our OpenCourseWare recording

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.480 align:middle line:90%
from 2013?

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:18.340 align:middle line:90%
Oh, great.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:20.980 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I see five or six hands.

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:25.320 align:middle line:84%
So you knew something
about this class.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:29.960 align:middle line:84%
How many of you have
done internship jobs

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:33.560 align:middle line:90%
at the financial firm?

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:34.480 align:middle line:90%
A third.

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:36.080 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.480 align:middle line:90%
How many of you are math majors?

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:40.840 align:middle line:90%
Most of you.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:44.360 align:middle line:84%
How many of you are
graduate students?

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:46.160 align:middle line:90%
OK, a few.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.700 align:middle line:84%
How many of you actually
are outside MIT?

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:50.360 align:middle line:90%
Because we have-- OK, great.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.640 align:middle line:84%
I want to welcome
all the students that

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:57.050 align:middle line:84%
came from cross registration
or sit-in from other schools.

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:00.670 align:middle line:84%
We always have a few
from other schools.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:02.110 align:middle line:90%
So you're very welcome.

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:06.190 align:middle line:84%
If you're unfamiliar with
some of the MIT systems,

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:10.130 align:middle line:84%
please feel free
to seek for help.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:13.110 align:middle line:84%
So that's all about
the introduction.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:17.570 align:middle line:84%
So let me get into the actual
content for this lecture.

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:20.850 align:middle line:84%
So as I said, the purpose
of this first lecture

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:22.710 align:middle line:84%
is to give you some
background information.

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:27.080 align:middle line:84%
I realize some of you already
have done intern jobs.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.210 align:middle line:90%
So you know quite a bit.

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:34.010 align:middle line:84%
But I want to also be
sensitive to the students who

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:38.690 align:middle line:84%
have actually not done
the internship positions.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:41.370 align:middle line:84%
And I can give you
some basic knowledge.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:44.530 align:middle line:90%
And I give you one example.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:49.632 align:middle line:84%
One of the first years when we
had this class, so there were

00:07:49.632 --> 00:07:51.090 align:middle line:84%
some students didn't
even know what

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:53.920 align:middle line:90%
a stock is, what a stock was.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:58.700 align:middle line:84%
So you shouldn't feel bad
if you have no knowledge

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:00.660 align:middle line:90%
about the financial markets.

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:04.260 align:middle line:84%
We will make sure you
have plenty of knowledge

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:07.630 align:middle line:84%
when you finish the
class in December.

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:11.200 align:middle line:90%


00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:17.580 align:middle line:84%
So what is finance or
financial markets about?

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:23.900 align:middle line:84%
It's really about-- there
is a market of exchanges

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:25.660 align:middle line:90%
in the original form.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:31.220 align:middle line:84%
People come to the market, at
the village, exchanging goods.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:32.919 align:middle line:90%
You have something.

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:34.080 align:middle line:90%
I don't have something.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.299 align:middle line:90%
I need something.

00:08:35.299 --> 00:08:37.260 align:middle line:90%
So let's exchange.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:42.980 align:middle line:84%
So the Stock Exchange was
formed with the same idea.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:47.460 align:middle line:84%
So some buyers want to
own equity of a company.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.300 align:middle line:90%
I'll explain what equity is.

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:54.420 align:middle line:84%
Equity is basically ownership
of a company, meaning stocks.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:58.700 align:middle line:84%
And they centralize
all the trading

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.340 align:middle line:84%
of these stocks at
a particular place.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:03.820 align:middle line:90%
These are exchanges.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:06.540 align:middle line:84%
Many cities, large
financial centers,

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:09.740 align:middle line:90%
have different exchanges.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:14.100 align:middle line:84%
And in the last few decades,
that also has changed.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.940 align:middle line:90%
It's no longer that centralized.

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:21.700 align:middle line:84%
So people do a lot of
over-the-counter, OTC, trading.

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:25.400 align:middle line:84%
So it's trade by appointment,
trade by arrangement,

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:26.800 align:middle line:90%
trade by contract.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:30.860 align:middle line:84%
So you don't have to go
to a centralized exchange

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:34.180 align:middle line:90%
to finish a trade.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:38.580 align:middle line:84%
Or there are electronic
communication networks, or ECNs.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.340 align:middle line:84%
You can do that
simply electronically.

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:45.980 align:middle line:84%
I mean, they also act as
distributed exchanges.

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:51.430 align:middle line:84%
So these are the major
platforms of markets.

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:54.070 align:middle line:84%
So what are the products
people actually are

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:56.670 align:middle line:90%
trading or exchanging hands?

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:59.710 align:middle line:90%
First is simply money.

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:02.250 align:middle line:90%
So Peter mentioned Bitcoin.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:05.470 align:middle line:84%
Bitcoin is actually kind
of a form of money--

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:09.550 align:middle line:84%
not the fiat form,
but different forms.

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:17.150 align:middle line:84%
But originally, gold, or metal,
or paper money, or coins,

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:22.470 align:middle line:84%
but now different currencies
issued by different governments.

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:29.190 align:middle line:84%
Those were basically the first
way of-- first essential product

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:31.550 align:middle line:90%
to trade with.

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:35.810 align:middle line:84%
Then you always hear
about the stock market.

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:39.450 align:middle line:84%
Stock market represents
ownership of companies.

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:46.350 align:middle line:84%
Peter mentioned NVIDIA stock
going up rapidly because people

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:50.280 align:middle line:84%
bet on AI applications,
the demand for GPU chips,

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:52.760 align:middle line:90%
and so on, data centers.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:57.480 align:middle line:84%
So to get ownership of a
company, you buy stocks.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:01.920 align:middle line:84%
I mean, also, you can
buy equity indices,

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:05.080 align:middle line:90%
meaning a basket of stocks.

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:10.030 align:middle line:84%
So you probably have
heard of S&P 500.

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:16.600 align:middle line:84%
So Standard & Poor's 500
is essentially five largest

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:20.600 align:middle line:90%
companies here.

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:25.400 align:middle line:84%
So you can get exposure not
to just simply one company,

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:29.880 align:middle line:84%
you can get exposure to many
of them at the same time.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:33.080 align:middle line:90%
These are the ideas of index.

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:40.120 align:middle line:84%
Or they can have different
forms of that traded in ETF,

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:43.920 align:middle line:84%
exchange traded funds,
also representing often

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:47.480 align:middle line:90%
a basket of different stocks.

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:53.440 align:middle line:84%
And there are also
two tiers of markets.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:59.000 align:middle line:84%
So the exchange traded stocks
is called the listed stocks.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.080 align:middle line:84%
So they have to go
through a process called

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:07.870 align:middle line:84%
IPO, initial public offering, to
become listed on the exchange.

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:10.440 align:middle line:90%


00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:13.840 align:middle line:84%
But that's called
the primary process

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:18.160 align:middle line:84%
to go from a private company
to become a public company,

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:20.680 align:middle line:90%
have stocks freely traded.

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:22.380 align:middle line:84%
There are also
private companies,

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:23.760 align:middle line:90%
which I'll get into later.

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:27.160 align:middle line:84%
But after it
becomes listed, it's

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:29.260 align:middle line:90%
traded by many other people.

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:31.480 align:middle line:84%
So it's called the
secondary market.

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:36.000 align:middle line:84%
So after primary, getting
listed, it's a secondary market.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:40.960 align:middle line:84%
So besides the large
category of equity market,

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:45.880 align:middle line:84%
there's also a big category of
bond or fixed income market.

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:48.250 align:middle line:90%
So bond is essentially a loan.

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:50.370 align:middle line:90%
It's a securitized loan.

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:53.410 align:middle line:84%
So it becomes
listed and tradable.

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:58.890 align:middle line:84%
So the most commonly known
bond is the US Treasury bond.

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:05.250 align:middle line:84%
US government borrows money
from the investors, us

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:07.490 align:middle line:90%
or institutions.

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:11.450 align:middle line:84%
They pay interest
periodically, either quarterly

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:13.130 align:middle line:90%
or semiannually.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:17.490 align:middle line:84%
And so the bond issuer
has an obligation

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:22.730 align:middle line:84%
to pay back the whole principal
amount, or the notional amount,

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:24.970 align:middle line:90%
at the end of the maturity.

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:28.610 align:middle line:84%
But they can also
default on that payment,

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:31.410 align:middle line:84%
on both the principal
or the interest.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:36.930 align:middle line:84%
So bond has a credit risk,
meaning that the issuer may not

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:38.730 align:middle line:90%
always pay back.

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:44.850 align:middle line:84%
So that involves the exposure
of the credit of the issuer.

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:50.780 align:middle line:84%
So that's basically related to
a whole bunch of credit related

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:52.300 align:middle line:90%
products.

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:58.540 align:middle line:84%
And 2008, global
financial crisis

00:13:58.540 --> 00:14:02.060 align:middle line:84%
is really a credit
crisis because that

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:05.540 align:middle line:90%
started in the mortgage market.

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:09.140 align:middle line:84%
People borrowed money to buy
houses, but couldn't pay back.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:12.660 align:middle line:84%
And also, these mortgages were
restructuring the different kind

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:14.860 align:middle line:90%
of derivative products.

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:18.060 align:middle line:90%
And we'll get into that later.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:22.160 align:middle line:84%
So related to bonds, there's
an interest rate curve,

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:24.910 align:middle line:90%
which is also very important.

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:28.140 align:middle line:84%
It depends on
maturity of the bonds.

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:33.540 align:middle line:84%
The interest payment, or the
coupon, is actually different.

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:36.220 align:middle line:84%
If you only borrow
for three months,

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:38.060 align:middle line:90%
you pay certain interest.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:41.660 align:middle line:84%
You borrow for 30 years, you
pay another interest rate

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:42.340 align:middle line:90%
every year.

00:14:42.340 --> 00:14:46.540 align:middle line:84%
It depends on the curve
shape of the interest rate,

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:50.620 align:middle line:84%
as well as the supply demand
and the many other factors.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:55.660 align:middle line:84%
The Federal Reserve bank, which
is the policy bank of the United

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:59.060 align:middle line:84%
States, is
responsible of setting

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:03.220 align:middle line:84%
the short term, the
front-end interest rate

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:07.300 align:middle line:84%
to affect the whole
interest rate curve.

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:09.286 align:middle line:84%
I mean, right now,
as many of you know,

00:15:09.286 --> 00:15:12.420 align:middle line:84%
the interest rate curve
is actually inverted.

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:16.820 align:middle line:84%
The front-end interest rate is
higher than the longer maturity,

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:19.660 align:middle line:90%
which is not often the case.

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:23.140 align:middle line:90%
We can also get into that later.

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:30.180 align:middle line:84%
So related to interest rates,
there are real estate products,

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:35.260 align:middle line:84%
as I mentioned, mortgage
and asset backed securities.

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:38.060 align:middle line:84%
And another whole
category is commodities.

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:39.540 align:middle line:90%
I mentioned gold.

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:41.700 align:middle line:90%
But there's also crude oil--

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:43.870 align:middle line:84%
Peter's example of the
futures dipping down

00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:48.710 align:middle line:84%
below $0 and
agricultural products.

00:15:48.710 --> 00:15:54.090 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're in the era of
electrifying everything.

00:15:54.090 --> 00:16:01.950 align:middle line:84%
Electric vehicles uses a lot of
copper or lithium for batteries.

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:06.950 align:middle line:84%
So those commodities are
trading rapidly higher

00:16:06.950 --> 00:16:10.390 align:middle line:90%
and also follows the cycle.

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:14.690 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just giving you some
ideas of the basic products.

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:17.170 align:middle line:84%
But on top of all
these products,

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:20.290 align:middle line:84%
you can also have
derivative products,

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:26.670 align:middle line:84%
meaning you can derive
other contracts of trading.

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:28.330 align:middle line:90%
So you can have options.

00:16:28.330 --> 00:16:30.830 align:middle line:84%
So I don't just buy
the stock, I want

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:34.510 align:middle line:84%
to buy the right to own
the stock in the future.

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:36.210 align:middle line:90%
That is the option.

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:39.270 align:middle line:84%
So it's the right, but
not the obligation.

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:41.330 align:middle line:84%
I can also say, I don't
want to buy it now.

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:43.800 align:middle line:84%
I just want to buy
it a year from now.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:46.240 align:middle line:90%
So that's a forward contract.

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:50.080 align:middle line:84%
So I just want to buy
the stock index futures,

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:54.480 align:middle line:84%
which is a very liquid
and standardized contract.

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:56.988 align:middle line:84%
So you can also say,
I want to do a swap.

00:16:56.988 --> 00:16:58.280 align:middle line:90%
I don't want really buy things.

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:00.540 align:middle line:84%
I want to say I buy
it from you now.

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:03.200 align:middle line:84%
But a month later, I
sell it back to you.

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:05.640 align:middle line:90%
So it's a swap contract.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:07.880 align:middle line:84%
And so there are
many different ways

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:13.819 align:middle line:84%
of structuring a product for
different purposes and needs.

00:17:13.819 --> 00:17:17.740 align:middle line:84%
So once you actually
land on a job like that,

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:19.099 align:middle line:90%
you become an expert.

00:17:19.099 --> 00:17:21.960 align:middle line:90%
Your math will be very useful.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:26.760 align:middle line:84%
So who are the
players in the market?

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:31.320 align:middle line:84%
So typically, they are the
banks you deposit money with--

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:34.120 align:middle line:90%
Bank of America, Chase.

00:17:34.120 --> 00:17:35.500 align:middle line:90%
They take deposits.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:39.520 align:middle line:84%
They also lend out the
money to home builders

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:42.680 align:middle line:90%
or other commercial projects.

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:45.140 align:middle line:84%
They are the investment
banks where you interview.

00:17:45.140 --> 00:17:47.840 align:middle line:90%
They come to MIT to recruit--

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:51.140 align:middle line:84%
so the Morgan Stanley's, the
Goldman Sachs, and others,

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:52.880 align:middle line:90%
JP Morgan's.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:55.800 align:middle line:84%
JP Morgan was actually
a commercial bank.

00:17:55.800 --> 00:18:03.240 align:middle line:84%
But since the termination of
Glass-Steagall law in 1999,

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:07.200 align:middle line:84%
they turned themselves into an
investment bank-- they turned

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:11.160 align:middle line:84%
themselves back into investment
bank because, many years ago,

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:16.670 align:middle line:84%
before 1930, Morgan Stanley
and JP Morgan were one company.

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:19.560 align:middle line:90%


00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:25.160 align:middle line:84%
So they have essentially three
main divisions in the investment

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:26.640 align:middle line:90%
banking business.

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:28.320 align:middle line:90%
One is called equity.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:30.520 align:middle line:84%
So when you go
interview, if you're

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:32.420 align:middle line:84%
interviewing with the
equity department,

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:36.600 align:middle line:84%
you should know that
that's to do with stocks.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:40.360 align:middle line:84%
When you're interviewing with
the fixed income department,

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:43.250 align:middle line:84%
you're going to deal
with bonds, interest rate

00:18:43.250 --> 00:18:47.330 align:middle line:84%
curves, currencies,
possibly commodities,

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:49.130 align:middle line:90%
and other derivatives.

00:18:49.130 --> 00:18:52.930 align:middle line:84%
You can also join the so-called
IBD, investment banking

00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:56.930 align:middle line:84%
division, which is more
about corporate finance

00:18:56.930 --> 00:19:01.130 align:middle line:84%
or raising money, taking a
company from private to be

00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:04.610 align:middle line:84%
a listed, public
company or issuing

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:06.510 align:middle line:90%
bonds or some other ways.

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:12.970 align:middle line:84%
So they are doing more services,
and the client relationship

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:20.050 align:middle line:84%
planning, and other type of
investment banking services.

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:23.710 align:middle line:84%
So beyond the investment
banking business of these banks,

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:26.770 align:middle line:84%
they usually have asset
management division.

00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:32.850 align:middle line:84%
So they produce products,
such as funds, mutual funds,

00:19:32.850 --> 00:19:36.930 align:middle line:84%
or other type of
higher return funds.

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:40.520 align:middle line:84%
They also provide wealth
management services,

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:45.260 align:middle line:84%
helping the individual
or wealthy families

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:49.140 align:middle line:90%
to manage their wealth.

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:53.460 align:middle line:84%
So outside the banks,
there are hedge funds.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:56.500 align:middle line:84%
So some of the internships
must be with some hedge funds.

00:19:56.500 --> 00:20:00.020 align:middle line:84%
Hedge funds are essentially
trading entities.

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:04.420 align:middle line:84%
So they use their capital
or the investor's capital

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:06.443 align:middle line:90%
to try to generate returns.

00:20:06.443 --> 00:20:07.860 align:middle line:84%
So by the way, at
the end of this,

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:12.700 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to assign a game to
all of you, which is related

00:20:12.700 --> 00:20:16.700 align:middle line:90%
to investing and trading

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:19.020 align:middle line:84%
So retail investors,
so they just

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:20.800 align:middle line:84%
simply open a
brokerage account--

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:26.100 align:middle line:84%
and you probably have and
your parents have-- and trade.

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:29.100 align:middle line:90%
Central banks, they involve--

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:31.660 align:middle line:84%
they're guiding the
markets, usually

00:20:31.660 --> 00:20:35.780 align:middle line:84%
providing liquidity, or
intervening the currencies,

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:36.320 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:38.430 align:middle line:90%
They are policy driven.

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:39.910 align:middle line:90%
There are corporates.

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:42.730 align:middle line:84%
So corporates come in
mostly for hedging purposes.

00:20:42.730 --> 00:20:49.110 align:middle line:84%
They want to reduce the exposure
from the exports or imports.

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:51.590 align:middle line:84%
What is the objective
of all these players

00:20:51.590 --> 00:20:55.230 align:middle line:90%
involved in the markets?

00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:59.870 align:middle line:84%
In one sentence, they
try to maximize the gain

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:03.910 align:middle line:90%
while minimizing the losses.

00:21:03.910 --> 00:21:06.590 align:middle line:84%
Because financial
markets are uncertain--

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:10.350 align:middle line:84%
so you don't know, when
you initiate a trade,

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:14.510 align:middle line:84%
you will end up making
money or losing money.

00:21:14.510 --> 00:21:17.390 align:middle line:90%
So investors have the money.

00:21:17.390 --> 00:21:19.950 align:middle line:90%
They are seeking returns.

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:25.430 align:middle line:84%
The borrowers-- or you can
think about them as managers.

00:21:25.430 --> 00:21:27.390 align:middle line:90%
They actually need the money.

00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:30.070 align:middle line:90%
They can take the risk

00:21:30.070 --> 00:21:32.550 align:middle line:84%
So the trade between the
lender and the borrower

00:21:32.550 --> 00:21:34.470 align:middle line:84%
is essentially the
main driving force

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:36.830 align:middle line:84%
of all the financial
relationships.

00:21:36.830 --> 00:21:39.590 align:middle line:84%
You can always pair
them up this way.

00:21:39.590 --> 00:21:43.630 align:middle line:84%
And another example I'll
just quickly mention-- you've

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:46.990 align:middle line:84%
probably heard the
terms LP versus GP.

00:21:46.990 --> 00:21:48.770 align:middle line:90%
LP means limited partners.

00:21:48.770 --> 00:21:51.390 align:middle line:90%
GP means general partners.

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:56.470 align:middle line:84%
Or in simple words,
it's the investor

00:21:56.470 --> 00:21:58.350 align:middle line:90%
versus hedge fund manager.

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:00.270 align:middle line:90%
It's that relationship.

00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:03.850 align:middle line:84%
If you one day want
to raise a hedge fund,

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:05.890 align:middle line:84%
you need to deal
with your investors.

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:10.670 align:middle line:84%
So you need to sort out
these kind of relationships.

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:17.230 align:middle line:84%
So the trader types we can
categorize into the three.

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:19.150 align:middle line:84%
One is the hedgers,
like corporates.

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:21.150 align:middle line:90%
They want to reduce risk.

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:22.890 align:middle line:84%
And the second is
the market maker.

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:26.610 align:middle line:84%
Like a lot of the
bank trading desks,

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:31.270 align:middle line:84%
they make a buying price,
a selling price, or bid,

00:22:31.270 --> 00:22:32.910 align:middle line:90%
or an offer.

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:37.600 align:middle line:84%
Their objective is to earn that
spread, but not to take risk.

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:40.140 align:middle line:84%
Every time, they try
to earn that spread,

00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:43.240 align:middle line:84%
then quickly flatten
out the position.

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:46.080 align:middle line:84%
The last category
are the risk seekers,

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:49.680 align:middle line:84%
the proprietary traders,
or the fund managers.

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:55.320 align:middle line:84%
So they use their own
capital to initiate risk.

00:22:55.320 --> 00:23:01.160 align:middle line:84%
And hopefully, they are
betting right to make a return.

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:04.360 align:middle line:84%
So how is this all
related to math?

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:07.440 align:middle line:84%
Why do you need to
know all these things?

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:09.560 align:middle line:84%
I assume you're in
this class because you

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:13.720 align:middle line:84%
have some interest of
finding out how math can be

00:23:13.720 --> 00:23:17.480 align:middle line:90%
used in the financial markets.

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:20.960 align:middle line:84%
So the math is used also in
the three different ways.

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:25.320 align:middle line:84%
One is to come up
with a pricing model,

00:23:25.320 --> 00:23:28.340 align:middle line:84%
because these instruments,
especially the derivatives,

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:32.160 align:middle line:84%
the options, the
forwards, the swaps

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:35.490 align:middle line:90%
is actually quite complicated.

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:38.430 align:middle line:84%
In one of the guest lectures
later on in the term,

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:40.690 align:middle line:90%
you will hear from John Hall.

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:47.450 align:middle line:84%
He wrote a textbook many years
ago on how to price options.

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:53.690 align:middle line:84%
Vasili and I probably
were, back then,

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:57.650 align:middle line:84%
hungry readers of those
books to figure out

00:23:57.650 --> 00:24:00.530 align:middle line:84%
how differential
equations can be used.

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:03.130 align:middle line:84%
I mean, he was a
pioneer in this field.

00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:10.010 align:middle line:84%
And MIT actually is was at
the frontier of all these

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:13.010 align:middle line:90%
innovations back in the '70s.

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:15.650 align:middle line:84%
So you probably heard
the Nobel Prize given

00:24:15.650 --> 00:24:19.770 align:middle line:84%
in the '90s to the
Black-Scholes-Merton model.

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:25.450 align:middle line:84%
So that was a model using
mathematics to price options.

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:31.730 align:middle line:84%
So they won an economic price,
Nobel Prize in economics.

00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:34.010 align:middle line:84%
So that's the
pricing model side.

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:36.570 align:middle line:84%
The second part is
also very important.

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:38.530 align:middle line:90%
It's risk management.

00:24:38.530 --> 00:24:44.290 align:middle line:84%
I mean, when you take on the
risk, take on a position,

00:24:44.290 --> 00:24:45.930 align:middle line:84%
the first question
you have to decide

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:49.370 align:middle line:84%
is how big the
position should be.

00:24:49.370 --> 00:24:51.690 align:middle line:84%
So you have a certain
amount of money.

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:55.690 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to put all in
all your chips on one stock?

00:24:55.690 --> 00:25:00.370 align:middle line:84%
Or do you want diversify, put
them into different stocks?

00:25:00.370 --> 00:25:04.810 align:middle line:84%
Once you put your investment in,
when do you change your mind?

00:25:04.810 --> 00:25:06.170 align:middle line:90%
When do you sell?

00:25:06.170 --> 00:25:07.350 align:middle line:90%
When do you take profit?

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:09.770 align:middle line:90%
When do you cut loss?

00:25:09.770 --> 00:25:13.170 align:middle line:90%
So all the things--

00:25:13.170 --> 00:25:16.450 align:middle line:84%
your greed, your
emotions-- will drive you.

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:22.570 align:middle line:84%
On one hand, your fear will also
drive you in other situations.

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:26.410 align:middle line:84%
So risk management is actually
a very quantitative process.

00:25:26.410 --> 00:25:31.490 align:middle line:84%
You should let math
speak for that process.

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:33.740 align:middle line:84%
The last one is, I
think, most people

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:37.420 align:middle line:84%
are interested in, especially
sitting in your seats.

00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:42.900 align:middle line:84%
If you can make a trading
strategy, perpetual money making

00:25:42.900 --> 00:25:46.900 align:middle line:84%
machine, using some kind
of a hidden mathematics,

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:48.200 align:middle line:90%
wouldn't that be so cool?

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:52.800 align:middle line:84%
You just go to the beach and let
the machine make money for you.

00:25:52.800 --> 00:25:56.700 align:middle line:84%
I mean, that's the dream of
many quantitative managers.

00:25:56.700 --> 00:26:00.300 align:middle line:84%
And I would say some of
them are very successful.

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:02.780 align:middle line:84%
Some of the funds
out there, they

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:06.700 align:middle line:84%
have achieved amazing
outcomes using mathematics.

00:26:06.700 --> 00:26:09.820 align:middle line:84%
But the problem is, once
they found those secrets,

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:12.220 align:middle line:90%
they would never disclose it.

00:26:12.220 --> 00:26:15.540 align:middle line:84%
And if you join them, they
will ask you to sign a lifetime

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:19.100 align:middle line:84%
non-compete, meaning,
when you leave the shop,

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:21.840 align:middle line:84%
you cannot say a word
of what you learned.

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:22.720 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, go ahead.

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:24.553 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: If you sign a
lifetime non-compete,

00:26:24.553 --> 00:26:25.970 align:middle line:84%
don't they have
to pay settlements

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:27.360 align:middle line:90%
when they fire you ultimately?

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:27.943 align:middle line:90%
JAKE XIA: Yes.

00:26:27.943 --> 00:26:28.980 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: For a lifetime?

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:31.000 align:middle line:84%
JAKE XIA: Not a
lifetime, unfortunately.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:33.890 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so we're not going
to get into that for this.

00:26:33.890 --> 00:26:36.960 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, but first step, you
have to find those secrets.

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:41.870 align:middle line:90%


00:26:41.870 --> 00:26:43.770 align:middle line:84%
So how do you find
those secrets?

00:26:43.770 --> 00:26:47.470 align:middle line:84%
So people say, OK, why don't
you just look at the data,

00:26:47.470 --> 00:26:52.150 align:middle line:84%
learn from the historical
patterns, then extrapolate them?

00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:57.870 align:middle line:84%
The efficient market theory
says that's not possible.

00:26:57.870 --> 00:27:02.090 align:middle line:84%
If there is such thing in $100
bills lying on the ground,

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:05.590 align:middle line:84%
they will already have been
picked up a long time ago.

00:27:05.590 --> 00:27:10.510 align:middle line:84%
Why would that secret be
waiting for you to uncover?

00:27:10.510 --> 00:27:13.310 align:middle line:84%
There are many smart
people before you.

00:27:13.310 --> 00:27:15.430 align:middle line:84%
The behavioral
finance theory says,

00:27:15.430 --> 00:27:19.750 align:middle line:84%
oh, wait a second, because
people are not totally rational.

00:27:19.750 --> 00:27:22.810 align:middle line:84%
Under certain situations,
we're not thinking rationally.

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:25.830 align:middle line:84%
That's why we're providing
these opportunities for others

00:27:25.830 --> 00:27:27.590 align:middle line:90%
to explore.

00:27:27.590 --> 00:27:29.280 align:middle line:90%
Is this a zero sum game?

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:32.510 align:middle line:90%


00:27:32.510 --> 00:27:37.710 align:middle line:84%
So I'm getting into now
investment strategies.

00:27:37.710 --> 00:27:40.830 align:middle line:84%
I'm not, of course, telling
you all the secrets.

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:44.070 align:middle line:84%
But I'm laying all
the steps for you

00:27:44.070 --> 00:27:46.990 align:middle line:84%
to think about what
you should focus on.

00:27:46.990 --> 00:27:49.950 align:middle line:84%
So firstly, you
should focus on, do

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:51.370 align:middle line:90%
you want to do this by yourself?

00:27:51.370 --> 00:27:53.830 align:middle line:84%
Or do you want to hire other
people to do it for you?

00:27:53.830 --> 00:27:57.910 align:middle line:84%
So this is the direct versus
investing in other people's

00:27:57.910 --> 00:28:01.110 align:middle line:90%
funds or fund of funds.

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:04.850 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to focus on
publicly traded markets?

00:28:04.850 --> 00:28:08.710 align:middle line:84%
As I said, all these things--
stocks, bonds, currencies.

00:28:08.710 --> 00:28:12.610 align:middle line:84%
Or you probably interviewed
or heard venture capital,

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:17.150 align:middle line:84%
private equity, buyouts, real
estate funds, natural resource

00:28:17.150 --> 00:28:18.030 align:middle line:90%
funds.

00:28:18.030 --> 00:28:20.290 align:middle line:84%
I mean, they're
not so transparent.

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:23.550 align:middle line:84%
But they seem to make
a lot of returns, too.

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:24.490 align:middle line:90%
You need to pick.

00:28:24.490 --> 00:28:28.270 align:middle line:84%
These are two very
different markets.

00:28:28.270 --> 00:28:31.440 align:middle line:90%
Also, passive versus active.

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:36.040 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to do the stock
picking, selecting the stocks?

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:38.920 align:middle line:84%
Or do you just want
to buy the index?

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:41.840 align:middle line:84%
Buying S&P 500 in
the last 10 years

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:44.680 align:middle line:84%
would have done very
well and beating

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:47.640 align:middle line:90%
most of the fund managers.

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:52.970 align:middle line:84%
So why shouldn't you
just do the passive?

00:28:52.970 --> 00:28:56.000 align:middle line:90%


00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:59.280 align:middle line:84%
Also, systematic
versus discretionary.

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:00.920 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to make
decisions based

00:29:00.920 --> 00:29:04.840 align:middle line:84%
on some systematic or
quantitative rules or models?

00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:08.320 align:middle line:84%
Or every time, do you want to
make a decision by yourself

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:11.920 align:middle line:90%
using your human brain?

00:29:11.920 --> 00:29:16.720 align:middle line:84%
Do you even you want to follow
the quantitative model using

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:18.120 align:middle line:90%
math?

00:29:18.120 --> 00:29:21.680 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to look for
statistical patterns

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:25.320 align:middle line:84%
or some deterministic
relationships?

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:29.210 align:middle line:84%
Deterministic relationships,
like a lot of physics.

00:29:29.210 --> 00:29:34.450 align:middle line:84%
If A plus B, you will get
C But in statistics, you

00:29:34.450 --> 00:29:38.130 align:middle line:84%
will have a probability
of getting C.

00:29:38.130 --> 00:29:43.170 align:middle line:84%
So I would say, nowadays,
finding deterministic arbitrage

00:29:43.170 --> 00:29:46.090 align:middle line:84%
or trading strategies
is very hard.

00:29:46.090 --> 00:29:50.330 align:middle line:84%
Those $100 bills have
definitely been picked up.

00:29:50.330 --> 00:29:53.690 align:middle line:84%
But sometimes,
they still show up.

00:29:53.690 --> 00:29:59.210 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to train
following or mean reversion?

00:29:59.210 --> 00:30:03.010 align:middle line:84%
That means, when market goes
up, do you want to buy more?

00:30:03.010 --> 00:30:04.990 align:middle line:84%
Or market goes up,
do you want to sell?

00:30:04.990 --> 00:30:10.210 align:middle line:84%
You play the contrarian
or you buy the dip.

00:30:10.210 --> 00:30:14.690 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to focus on short
term or you focus on long term?

00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:17.170 align:middle line:84%
Short term, you're
trading very fast

00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:19.630 align:middle line:90%
and you're trading the liquid.

00:30:19.630 --> 00:30:22.050 align:middle line:90%
You can change your mind easily.

00:30:22.050 --> 00:30:26.030 align:middle line:84%
Or you make your
decisions very slowly.

00:30:26.030 --> 00:30:27.700 align:middle line:90%
You take your time.

00:30:27.700 --> 00:30:32.180 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the start up companies
you invest with your venture

00:30:32.180 --> 00:30:35.300 align:middle line:84%
capital may take 10 years to
find out if it's successful

00:30:35.300 --> 00:30:37.860 align:middle line:90%
or not.

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:41.420 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to be a Warren
Buffett, like value investor?

00:30:41.420 --> 00:30:44.420 align:middle line:84%
You look at the
current price should

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:47.340 align:middle line:90%
be much lower than its value.

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:50.500 align:middle line:84%
Or you focus on the
growth of the company.

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:54.740 align:middle line:84%
You project NVIDIA'S future
income for the next 10 years.

00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:57.740 align:middle line:84%
As long as the
future value is going

00:30:57.740 --> 00:31:03.060 align:middle line:84%
to be greater than the current
value, you can invest in it.

00:31:03.060 --> 00:31:05.280 align:middle line:84%
I would say, in
the last few years,

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:06.820 align:middle line:84%
more and more
people are focusing

00:31:06.820 --> 00:31:09.300 align:middle line:90%
on growth rather than value.

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:11.460 align:middle line:90%
You need to figure out why.

00:31:11.460 --> 00:31:16.040 align:middle line:90%
And history versus future.

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:18.980 align:middle line:84%
Do you believe history
will repeat itself,

00:31:18.980 --> 00:31:21.780 align:middle line:90%
so you follow the patterns?

00:31:21.780 --> 00:31:28.700 align:middle line:84%
Or you actually don't simply
extrapolate into the future.

00:31:28.700 --> 00:31:32.540 align:middle line:84%
The key is you need to
understand your own objectives.

00:31:32.540 --> 00:31:35.680 align:middle line:84%
So your objective is
to maximize your gain

00:31:35.680 --> 00:31:38.460 align:middle line:90%
versus minimizing your loss.

00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:42.540 align:middle line:90%
But that's not all of it.

00:31:42.540 --> 00:31:48.180 align:middle line:84%
You need to understand
why do you need the money.

00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:49.700 align:middle line:90%
What's it for?

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:52.340 align:middle line:90%
How much can you afford to lose?

00:31:52.340 --> 00:31:55.900 align:middle line:84%
How much are you going
to spend every year?

00:31:55.900 --> 00:32:01.300 align:middle line:84%
And your life horizon of
income versus spending, this

00:32:01.300 --> 00:32:03.860 align:middle line:84%
is a whole set of
investment objectives

00:32:03.860 --> 00:32:06.220 align:middle line:84%
you need to understand
about yourself.

00:32:06.220 --> 00:32:10.380 align:middle line:84%
Then you need to pick
an area you're the best.

00:32:10.380 --> 00:32:13.420 align:middle line:84%
You have an edge over
the rest of the market.

00:32:13.420 --> 00:32:16.000 align:middle line:84%
You need to understand
why you should make money.

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:18.380 align:middle line:84%
Other people should
give you the money.

00:32:18.380 --> 00:32:21.880 align:middle line:84%
And you need to find a
suitable strategy for you.

00:32:21.880 --> 00:32:26.210 align:middle line:84%
Ultimately, if math
is your strength,

00:32:26.210 --> 00:32:28.570 align:middle line:90%
you need to make math your edge.

00:32:28.570 --> 00:32:31.190 align:middle line:90%


00:32:31.190 --> 00:32:31.830 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:32:31.830 --> 00:32:36.470 align:middle line:84%
For that, I'm going to quickly
talk about the trading game.

00:32:36.470 --> 00:32:38.990 align:middle line:90%
Then I will pass on to Vasili.

00:32:38.990 --> 00:32:43.870 align:middle line:84%
So this game is about, for
every single one of you,

00:32:43.870 --> 00:32:50.470 align:middle line:84%
to pick a stock or ETF, exchange
traded funds, just traded

00:32:50.470 --> 00:32:55.090 align:middle line:84%
like stock, or bonds,
or whatever only to buy.

00:32:55.090 --> 00:32:57.050 align:middle line:84%
So I know some of you
are more sophisticated.

00:32:57.050 --> 00:32:58.230 align:middle line:90%
So can I short?

00:32:58.230 --> 00:33:04.150 align:middle line:84%
If you want to go short, you
buy the short-related ETF.

00:33:04.150 --> 00:33:06.590 align:middle line:90%
So pick one stock.

00:33:06.590 --> 00:33:09.210 align:middle line:84%
You are hypothetically
given $10,000.

00:33:09.210 --> 00:33:11.650 align:middle line:84%
Everyone has $10,000,
each one of you.

00:33:11.650 --> 00:33:14.350 align:middle line:90%


00:33:14.350 --> 00:33:15.650 align:middle line:90%
You need to let us know.

00:33:15.650 --> 00:33:17.990 align:middle line:90%
Email us your selection.

00:33:17.990 --> 00:33:19.950 align:middle line:90%
You pick only one thing.

00:33:19.950 --> 00:33:23.390 align:middle line:84%
You let us know
before September 14.

00:33:23.390 --> 00:33:25.900 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to give you
the slide on the website.

00:33:25.900 --> 00:33:27.480 align:middle line:90%
So don't worry.

00:33:27.480 --> 00:33:33.440 align:middle line:84%
The price you buy is at the
close of Friday of September 13.

00:33:33.440 --> 00:33:35.720 align:middle line:84%
The price you sell
is automatically

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:41.600 align:middle line:84%
set on Friday, November 15,
so about a two-month period.

00:33:41.600 --> 00:33:44.840 align:middle line:84%
So you need to track on the
spreadsheet how many shares you

00:33:44.840 --> 00:33:45.620 align:middle line:90%
have bought.

00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:47.880 align:middle line:90%
That's easy. $10,000, right?

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:53.360 align:middle line:84%
You just convert into shares
divided by per share price.

00:33:53.360 --> 00:33:58.220 align:middle line:84%
And your daily profit and
loss in dollar and percentage,

00:33:58.220 --> 00:34:02.540 align:middle line:84%
that gives you a practice of
how to track your position.

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:06.360 align:middle line:84%
Every day, there's a
P&L, profit and loss.

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:09.239 align:middle line:84%
At the end of the
close on November 15,

00:34:09.239 --> 00:34:13.120 align:middle line:84%
calculate the total
loss or profit.

00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:16.800 align:middle line:84%
And also, I want you to do
one calculation to sum up

00:34:16.800 --> 00:34:21.760 align:middle line:84%
all the daily gains
on one side, called G,

00:34:21.760 --> 00:34:26.800 align:middle line:84%
and sum up all the daily losses,
called L, in dollar terms.

00:34:26.800 --> 00:34:30.719 align:middle line:84%
So G minus L should be
your net profit and loss.

00:34:30.719 --> 00:34:34.199 align:middle line:84%
But I want you to calculate
that ratio, G minus L divided

00:34:34.199 --> 00:34:37.600 align:middle line:84%
by G plus L. That gives you
the quality of your trading

00:34:37.600 --> 00:34:40.320 align:middle line:90%
strategy.

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:43.840 align:middle line:84%
A bonus-- you are given a
one-time chance to change

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:47.560 align:middle line:90%
your mind on October 11 close.

00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:49.260 align:middle line:90%
You email us your decision.

00:34:49.260 --> 00:34:52.880 align:middle line:84%
If you want to switch
before October 12,

00:34:52.880 --> 00:34:56.080 align:middle line:84%
you can switch to
one other position.

00:34:56.080 --> 00:34:57.580 align:middle line:90%
You can decide to not switch.

00:34:57.580 --> 00:35:01.440 align:middle line:84%
But when you switch, you carry
the balance, whatever is left.

00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:06.300 align:middle line:84%
Your $10,000, maybe $1,000
left or become $50,000,

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:09.000 align:middle line:84%
whatever roll into
a new position.

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:12.680 align:middle line:84%
So at the end of the term, we
will announce the top three

00:35:12.680 --> 00:35:13.920 align:middle line:90%
leaders.

00:35:13.920 --> 00:35:17.330 align:middle line:84%
OK, for that, I'll
pass it on to Vasili.

00:35:17.330 --> 00:35:23.000 align:middle line:90%